<p>For products that go off or can be recalled (tile adhesive, sealants, paint, chemicals, fertiliser), HardPoint can keep track of each
  manufacturer's batch and its expiry date.</p>

<h2>Turning it on</h2>
<p>Open the product, press <strong>Edit</strong> and tick <strong>Batches and expiry dates</strong>. Anything already on the shelf is kept as
  "not in a batch" and sold first.</p>

<h2>Receiving</h2>
<p>When the product arrives, enter the batch number and the expiry date printed on the packaging, on the goods received note or on the phone.
  A delivery with two batches of the same product is received as two lines (or two deliveries on the phone).</p>

<h2>Selling</h2>
<p>Nothing changes at the till. HardPoint takes stock from the batch that expires first, and skips expired batches unless nothing else is left.
  The sale remembers which batches it took; returns and voids put stock back into the same batches, and transfers carry the batch to the
  other branch.</p>

<h2>Expired stock</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Stock › Expiry</strong> lists what has expired or expires in the next 30 days at a branch, and what it's worth.</li>
  <li>Open a batch and press <strong>Write off</strong> to take expired or damaged stock out, so the shelf and HardPoint agree.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Recalls</h2>
<p>A batch's page lists <strong>who bought it</strong>, at every branch, with the customer's phone number where the sale was in their name.
  The product's page and each sale show the batches too.</p>

<h2>Stock takes</h2>
<p>In a stock take, products tracked by batch are counted <strong>batch by batch</strong>. The stock take lists each batch that was in stock
  when it started, plus a line for stock not in any batch. Enter what you find for each batch. If a pack has a batch number that isn't
  listed, use <strong>A batch not listed</strong> (or type the number when scanning) and add its expiry date. Batches you don't count are
  left as they are.</p>
<p>When a manager approves the stock take, each batch is corrected to what was counted. A batch found on the shelf is added with its
  expiry date, so it's sold in expiry order like the rest. If some of a batch was sold while the count was going on, it can't go below
  nothing; the rest of the difference comes off the product's other stock, oldest first.</p>
